Exercise 3.19, given approximable maps \(f:\mathcal{D}_0\to\mathcal{D}'_0\) and \(g:\mathcal{D}_1\to\mathcal{D}'_1\), asks for \(f\times g\) and \(f+g\) such that (i) \((f\times g)(x,y)=\langle f(x),g(y)\rangle\), (ii) \(f\times g=\langle f\circ p_0,g\circ p_1\rangle\), (iii) \(\mathrm{out}_0\circ(f+g)\circ\mathrm{in}_0=f\), and (iv) \(\mathrm{out}_1\circ(f+g)\circ\mathrm{in}_1=g\), and asks whether (iii) and (iv) uniquely determine \(f+g\). Clause (ii) is the definition `prodMap`, the pairing of \(f\) after the left projection with \(g\) after the right projection. `toElementMap_prodMap_pair` is (i): the element map of the pairing is the pair of the element maps, composition acts by composition of element maps, and the element maps of the projections are the two components, after which the components of a pair are the paired elements. `toElementMap_prodMap` is the same identity for a general product element, written with `fst` and `snd`, and `example_319` is the conjunction of these two element equations. `proj_paired` recovers each factor by projecting the pairing, and `paired_unique` says that an approximable map into a product which commutes with the two projections equals that pairing. The same closure also contains the functor laws `prodMap_id` and `prodMap_comp` (Exercise 3.20): both sides are compared on elements, and each side sends a product element to the pair of the iterated images of its components. For the sum, `sumMap` relates sum neighbourhoods when the output is the codomain master, or both are left copies and \(f\) relates the underlying neighbourhoods, or both are right copies and \(g\) does; the master clause gives the master relation, and closure under intersection and monotonicity use absorption by the master (`eq_sumMaster_of_subset`), injectivity of each copy (`mem_subset_inj₀`, `mem_subset_inj₁`), and the impossibility of placing a nonempty copy of one tag inside a copy of the other (`not_inj₀_subset_inj₁`, `not_inj₁_subset_inj₀`). Assuming every neighbourhood of the four systems is nonempty, so that the separated sum is defined, `outMap₀_comp_sumMap_comp_inMap₀` and `outMap₁_comp_sumMap_comp_inMap₁` are (iii) and (iv), conjoined as `example_319_sum`. Each is relation extensionality: if the composite relates an input to \(Z\), the intermediate sum neighbourhood is the master, a matching copy, or a crossed copy; the master forces \(Z\) to be the codomain master and monotonicity applies the master relation of \(f\) or \(g\); the matching copy supplies the inclusions to which monotonicity applies; the crossed copy would put a nonempty injected copy inside the other tag. Conversely, the tagged copies of the input and of \(Z\) witness the composite. No theorem in the closure states that (iii) and (iv) determine \(f+g\), nor that they fail to. **Theorem:** partial — (i)–(iv) match, with (ii) the definition of `prodMap`, and uniqueness of \(f+g\) is not a theorem. **Proof:** partial — the element calculation for the product and the case analysis on sum neighbourhoods for (iii) and (iv) are present, and non-uniqueness is not proved.
